// tang-pqc -- Tamarin model of the post-quantum Tang escrow protocol
// (crates/tang-pqc/DESIGN.md)
//
// Scope:
//   S3.1  server keys, the advertisement, the pinned signing-key
//         thumbprint (thp) and its verification
//   S3.2  provisioning: ML-KEM encapsulation, K and confirm derivation,
//         the stored binding (kid, thp, ct, r, confirm)
//   S3.3  recovery: the fresh transport encapsulation, the sealed
//         request and response, the confirm check
//   S4    the security analysis: P1 two-factor, P5 authenticated setup,
//         the sealed transport, and the documented P2 gap (a stolen
//         binding queries the server; disk + server yield K)
//
// Symbolic abstraction:
//   - ML-DSA and the advertisement are fact-driven: an advertisement is
//     the !Server fact, and a client provisions only when its pin names
//     that server's signing key -- S3.1's client trust check (the
//     signature verifies under the pinned thp, and the exchange key
//     comes from the same signed JWK Set).  A rogue pin matches only
//     the rogue server.
//   - ML-KEM  -> kem_ct(ek, s) binds a fresh secret s under
//     encapsulation key ek = kem_pk(seed); only kem_dec(kem_sk(seed), .)
//     recovers s.  Encapsulation is public: anyone may build kem_ct
//     under a known ek, as FIPS 203 allows.
//   - AES-256-GCM (one message per key, nonce 0) -> senc/sdec.
//   - HKDF/H -> h(.): K = h(<ss, r>), confirm = h(<ss, r, 'confirm'>),
//     transport keys h(<sst, 'req'>) and h(<sst, 'rsp'>).  The bound
//     fields (alg, kid, thp, H(ct)) are abstracted into the tuple
//     shapes; every stored field still feeds the derivation.
//   - Time is not modeled: the advertisement's iat/exp and the Policy
//     freshness checks are treated as unexpired (S3.1).
//   - The disk is a steal_disk rule: it gives the attacker the
//     binding's stored fields (ct, r, confirm), never K itself.
//   - The server answers two paths.  The client path matches the
//     request of a client's Pending state (a Req fact) and records the
//     sealed response in a Served fact the client consumes -- the
//     decryption-by-fact idiom, sound because only the key holder
//     creates the fact.  The query path is the disk holder's
//     documented capability (S4, "querying the server with a stolen
//     binding"): a StolenCt fact carries the stolen ciphertext, the
//     rule composes the querier's fresh transport and the server's
//     answer, and the querier learns its own transport secret (it
//     chose it), so it opens the response.  A malformed request leaves
//     a term the equations cannot reduce to anything openable (the
//     clean RecoveryFailed of S4).  The query rule's ss2 is bound by
//     the KEM equation through the stolen ciphertext's shape, which
//     the derivation checker cannot see -- the same expected warning
//     class as the kuipa model's documented KEM bindings.
//
// Running the model:
//   tamarin-prover --prove tang-pqc.spthy
//
// Results with tamarin-prover 1.12.0 and Maude 3.5.1:
//   Exec, DiskFactorNecessary, AdvAuthentic, SsSealed and
//   ResponseAuthentic: verified.
//   Attack_MisPinnedThpLeaksKey: falsified -- a rogue thp plus a stolen
//     disk yields K, since the rogue server's seed is public; P5's
//     out-of-band pin delivery is what the secrecy lemmas rest on.
//   Attack_StolenDiskQueriesServerForSs, Attack_StolenDiskAndServerYieldKey:
//     falsified -- the server answers a stolen binding (the sealed
//     transport hides ct from observers but cannot stop the disk
//     holder from using the server factor), so disk + server give ss
//     and then K.  That is the intended binding to server presence and
//     the P2 gap of S5; its mitigations (sss across servers, the
//     hybrid MR share) are out of scope here.

theory tang_pqc
begin

functions: vk_h/1, kem_pk/1, kem_sk/1, kem_ct/2, kem_dec/2, senc/2, sdec/2, h/1
equations: kem_dec(kem_sk(x), kem_ct(kem_pk(x), s)) = s,
           sdec(senc(m, k), k) = m

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Server setup (S3.1): one signing key (ML-DSA) and one exchange key
// (ML-KEM) per server.  The rogue server is the attacker's own; its
// seeds are public, so the attacker holds its dk and can sign with it.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule server_setup:
  [ Fr(~ssk), Fr(~jseed) ]
  --[ HonestKeyPair(vk_h(~ssk), kem_pk(~jseed)) ]->
  [ !Server($srv, ~ssk, vk_h(~ssk), ~jseed, kem_pk(~jseed)),
    !HonestKeys(vk_h(~ssk), ~jseed, kem_pk(~jseed)) ]

rule rogue_server:
  [ Fr(~ssk), Fr(~jseed) ]
  --[ RogueKeyPair(vk_h(~ssk), kem_pk(~jseed)) ]->
  [ !Server('rogue', ~ssk, vk_h(~ssk), ~jseed, kem_pk(~jseed)),
    Out(~ssk), Out(~jseed) ]

// GET /key/{kid}: the public exchange key, served for advertised and
// retired keys alike (S3.3, S6).  The client checks the thumbprint
// against the binding's kid, which recover_start enforces by
// unification; a substituted key never matches.
rule serve_key:
  [ !Server(srv, ssk, svk, jseed, ek) ]
  -->
  [ Out(<'key', ek>) ]

// The exchange key's private half leaks (a retired key kept on disk, an
// HSM breach): the attacker learns the seed, hence dk.
rule compromise_exchange_key:
  [ !HonestKeys(svk, jseed, ek) ]
  --[ ExchangeKeyCompromised(jseed, ek) ]->
  [ Out(jseed) ]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// The pin (S3.1): the trusted thp reaches the client out of band.
// pin_honest pins an honest server's signing key; pin_rogue pins the
// attacker's (a mis-delivered thp: a forged pin file, or a
// man-in-the-middle at provisioning time).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule pin_honest:
  [ !HonestKeys(svk, jseed, ek) ]
  --[ HonestPin(svk) ]->
  [ !Pin(svk) ]

rule pin_rogue:
  [ !Server('rogue', ssk, svk, jseed, ek) ]
  --[ RoguePin(vk_h(ssk)) ]->
  [ !Pin(vk_h(ssk)) ]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Provisioning (S3.2): the client verifies the advertisement against
// its pin, encapsulates to the advertised exchange key and derives
//
//   (ct, ss) = ML-KEM.Encaps(ek)
//   K        = h(<ss, r>)                  the protected key
//   confirm  = h(<ss, r, 'confirm'>)       stored on disk
//
// The binding stores (kid = h(ek), thp = svk, ct, r, confirm); K never
// touches the disk.  Every stored field is bound into the derivation
// through the tuple shapes of h(.).
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule provision:
  [ !Pin(vk_h(ssk)),
    !Server(srv, ssk, vk_h(ssk), jseed, ek),
    Fr(~b), Fr(~ss), Fr(~r) ]
  --[ Provisioned(~b, ek, vk_h(ssk), ~ss, h(<~ss, ~r>)) ]->
  [ !Bound(~b, h(ek), vk_h(ssk), ~ss, ~r, kem_ct(ek, ~ss),
             h(<~ss, ~r, 'confirm'>)) ]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Disk theft: the attacker copies the binding.  It holds ct, r and
// confirm -- everything on the disk -- and never K.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule steal_disk:
  [ !Bound(b, kid, svk, ss, r, ct, confirm) ]
  --[ StolenDisk(b) ]->
  [ StolenCt(ct), Out(ct), Out(r), Out(confirm) ]
  // StolenCt marks the binding ciphertexts the attacker holds; the
  // server query rule serves exactly those (the disk holder's
  // capability of S4), not every constructible ciphertext.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Recovery (S3.3): the client fetches the exchange key by kid, makes a
// fresh transport encapsulation and seals the binding's ct under
// k_req = h(<sst, 'req'>):
//
//   (ct_t, ss_t) = ML-KEM.Encaps(ek)
//   request      = <'rec', kid, ct_t, senc(ct, k_req)>
//
// The thumbprint check is the unification of the fetched key with the
// binding's kid (h(ek)); a substituted key never opens a request path.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

rule recover_start:
  [ !Bound(b, h(ek), svk, ss, r, ct, confirm),
    In(<'key', ek>),
    Fr(~sst) ]
  --[ RecoveryStarted(b) ]->
  [ Pending(b, ss, r, ~sst),
    Req(h(ek), ct, ~sst),
    Out(<'rec', h(ek), kem_ct(ek, ~sst), senc(ct, h(<~sst, 'req'>))>) ]
  // Req is the client's own request record: the binding ciphertext it
  // sealed and the transport secret it encapsulated.  The wire term
  // keeps the attacker's view (a fresh ct_t and a sealed body); the
  // server rule consumes Req for the honest path.

// The server answers any well-formed request for its key: it
// decapsulates the transport (the equations reduce the structural
// pattern's sst), opens the sealed body to the binding ciphertext ct2,
// decapsulates that, and seals the result under this request's
// response key.  The structural In pattern is the length checks and
// the transport-key derivation of S3.3; the Out term is what the
// equations leave for a well-formed request, and anything the attacker
// garbles reduces to an unopenable term instead (RecoveryFailed).
rule recover_server_client:
  [ !Server(srv, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    In(<'rec', kid, ct_t, body>),
    Req(kid, ct2, sst) ]
  --[ ServerRecovered(srv) ]->
  [ Served(kem_dec(kem_sk(jseed), ct2), h(<sst, 'rsp'>)) ]
  // The honest path: the request belongs to a client's Pending state
  // (the Req record), so its transport secret sst, binding ciphertext
  // ct2 and kid are known.  The server decapsulates ct2 with its
  // private seed and records the sealed response (the Served fact is
  // the idealized response ciphertext -- its key h(<sst,'rsp'>) is
  // derived from a transport secret only the client and the dk holder
  // know, so a network observer learns nothing from the term anyway).
  // The honest path: the request belongs to a client's Pending state
  // (the Req record), so its transport secret sst, binding ciphertext
  // ct2 and kid are known.  The wire tuple is matched shallowly; the
  // server decapsulates ct2 with its private seed and seals the answer
  // under this request's response key.
  // The honest path: the request belongs to a client's Pending state
  // (the Req record), so its transport secret sst and sealed body are
  // known.  The server decapsulates ct2 with its private seed and
  // seals the answer under this request's response key.

rule recover_server_query:
  [ !Server(srv, ssk, svk, jseed, ek),
    StolenCt(kem_ct(ek, ss2)),
    Fr(~sst) ]
  --[ ServerQueried(srv) ]->
  [ Out( ~sst ),
    Out( <'query', h(ek),
          senc(kem_ct(ek, ss2), h(<~sst, 'req'>)),
          senc(ss2, h(<~sst, 'rsp'>))> ) ]
  // The disk holder queries: it encapsulates a fresh transport secret,
  // seals the stolen binding ciphertext under the request key and
  // receives ss2 sealed under the response key.  The rule composes
  // both wire terms itself (the disk holder is the attacker), so the
  // proof search never enumerates attacker constructions; the Out pair
  // gives the attacker both what it sent and what it learned.
  // Out(~sst) is the querier's knowledge of its own encapsulation: it
  // chose the transport secret, so it can derive the response key and
  // open senc(ss2, ...) -- that is the documented capability, not an
  // extra one.
  // ss2 is bound by the equations: StolenCt carries the binding
  // ciphertext as a well-formed kem_ct under this server's key, and
  // only the dk holder (or the disk holder that stole it) can pair it
  // with its decapsulation.  S4 documents this capability: "querying
  // the server with a stolen binding ... the same as in MR".

// The client opens the response and checks confirm: only a response
// carrying exactly this binding's ss, sealed under this request's sst,
// completes recovery.  The In pattern is the constant-time confirm
// comparison; anything else is a clean RecoveryFailed.
rule recover_finish:
  [ Pending(b, ss, r, sst),
    In(rsp),
    Served(ss, h(<sst, 'rsp'>)) ]
  --[ Recovered(b, h(<ss, r>)) ]->
  [ ]
  // binding: rsp = senc(ss, h(<sst, 'rsp'>)); the client opens it
  // under the response key derived from its own transport secret sst
  // and checks confirm against the binding (S3.3).  The Served fact
  // binds the plaintext; the In(rsp) premise keeps the network term
  // honest, so the attacker cannot complete a recovery it only
  // overheard.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Lemmas
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// The protocol runs: a client pins an honest server, provisions and
// recovers its key through it.
lemma Exec: exists-trace
  "Ex b key #i. Recovered(b, key) @i"

// P1, the disk factor: whoever learns K has stolen the disk.  The
// server (even with dk compromised, even answering queries) never sees
// r, so the server factor alone never yields K -- S4's "server only"
// row.
lemma DiskFactorNecessary:
  "All b ek svk ss key #i #j.
    (Provisioned(b, ek, svk, ss, key) @i & K(key) @j)
    ==> (Ex #m. StolenDisk(b) @m)"

// P5, authenticated setup: a client whose pin is honest binds to that
// honest server's exchange key.  Neither a forged advertisement nor a
// substituted key set can move an honestly-pinned client onto another
// ek.
lemma AdvAuthentic:
  "All b ek svk ss key #i #k.
    (Provisioned(b, ek, svk, ss, key) @i & HonestPin(svk) @k)
    ==> (Ex #m. HonestKeyPair(svk, ek) @m)"

// The sealed transport (S3.3, S4 "recorded traffic"): for an honestly
// pinned binding, the shared secret ss leaks only through the disk or
// the exchange key's compromise.  A pure network observer -- even one
// recording every exchange -- gets neither ct nor ss: opening either
// sealed direction needs ss_t, which needs dk.
lemma SsSealed:
  "All b ek svk ss key #i #k #j.
    (Provisioned(b, ek, svk, ss, key) @i & HonestPin(svk) @k & K(ss) @j)
    ==> ( (Ex #m. StolenDisk(b) @m)
        | (Ex jseed #m. ExchangeKeyCompromised(jseed, ek) @m) )"

// The confirm check (S3.3, S4 "tampered or replayed recovery
// response"): for an honestly pinned binding, a recovery completes
// only when a dk holder answered, or the exchange key was fully
// compromised -- which lets the attacker derive both ss and ss_t and
// impersonate the server, and holding the server's key is holding the
// server.  confirm authenticates the server: only the holder of
// dk_kid can return the right ss.
lemma ResponseAuthentic:
  "All b ek svk ss key #i #k #r.
    (Provisioned(b, ek, svk, ss, key) @i & HonestPin(svk) @k
     & Recovered(b, key) @r)
    ==> ( (Ex srv #m. ServerRecovered(srv) @m)
        | (Ex jseed #m. ExchangeKeyCompromised(jseed, ek) @m) )"

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// Falsifiable checks (expected: falsified, i.e. attacks found).  They
// show the verified lemmas are conditioned on the honest pin and that
// the model keeps the attacker capabilities S4 and S5 document, so the
// proofs above are not vacuous.
// ------------------------------------------------------------------

// A mis-delivered thp (the rogue server's signing key) plus a stolen
// disk yields K: the rogue seed is public, so the attacker decapsulates
// ct from the disk without any server.  P5's out-of-band pin delivery
// is load-bearing.
lemma Attack_MisPinnedThpLeaksKey:
  "All b ek svk ss key #i #k #m.
    ( Provisioned(b, ek, svk, ss, key) @i & RoguePin(svk) @k
      & StolenDisk(b) @m )
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(key) @j)"

// A stolen binding queries the honest server (S4: "querying the server
// with a stolen binding ... the same as in MR"): the disk holder
// encapsulates afresh and seals the stolen ct, and the server answers,
// so ss reaches the attacker.  The sealed transport hides ct from
// observers, not from the disk holder that holds it.
lemma Attack_StolenDiskQueriesServerForSs:
  "All b ek svk ss key #i #k #m.
    ( Provisioned(b, ek, svk, ss, key) @i & HonestPin(svk) @k
      & StolenDisk(b) @m )
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(ss) @j)"

// Disk plus server yield K: Tang's intended binding to server presence
// and the practical face of the P2 gap (S5).  This is why the design
// recommends sss across servers or the hybrid MR share when one
// server's log must not be enough.
lemma Attack_StolenDiskAndServerYieldKey:
  "All b ek svk ss key #i #k #m.
    ( Provisioned(b, ek, svk, ss, key) @i & HonestPin(svk) @k
      & StolenDisk(b) @m )
    ==> not(Ex #j. K(key) @j)"

end
